--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party material
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

This repository ships no Mojang data and no Mojang source code, in any
form (SPEC.md section 12). Vanilla worldgen definitions and conformance
fixtures are obtained by the user, at build/test time, from an official
jar the user downloads themselves; they are never redistributed here.

"Minecraft" is a trademark of Mojang AB. This project is not affiliated
with, endorsed by, or associated with Mojang AB or Microsoft.

Code adapted from third-party projects carries per-file attribution
naming the upstream file and its license. Expected upstreams:

  * cubiomes  — MIT License        — noise/biome math reference
  * Cuberite  — Apache License 2.0 — pipeline architecture reference

Any such adaptation must be recorded here and in the file header before
it merges.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
cubiomes (MIT)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every adaptation below is recorded against cubiomes commit
e61f90580cbdd883214a8054670dacae655e59c0, the commit tools/vectors pins,
and was checked against the upstream text at that commit. Each adapted
file names its upstream file and licence in its header.

lib/src/blended.cpp is adapted from cubiomes by Cubitect
(https://github.com/Cubitect/cubiomes): the octave loop and blend of
`sampleSurfaceNoise` in biomenoise.c, and the octave seeding order of
`octaveInit` in noise.c. Its `maintainPrecision` is noise.h's function of
that name with the body noise.h keeps as a commented-out line,
`x - round(x / 33554432.0) * 33554432.0`: the name, the wrap into +/-2^25
and its period are adapted from that line. This project's own: the
rounding, half up as `floor(v / 2^25 + 0.5)`, where C's `round` takes a
half away from zero (the two part at every negative odd multiple of 2^24),
and applying the wrap at all, where cubiomes' function returns its argument
unchanged. spec/blended-noise-spec.md Q1.6, which arrived later (3e41d5f),
states the same wrap with the same half-up floor.
lib/include/stratum/noise/blended.hpp declares both and carries the same
attribution.

lib/src/perlin.cpp is adapted from noise.c, function by function:

  * the gradient tables and `gradientDot` are the three 16-entry edge
    tables of noise.c's disabled `indexedLerp` (its `#if 0` branch);
  * `perlinFrom` is `perlinInit` / `xPerlinInit`: three origin draws times
    256, then the identity table shuffled by `nextInt(256 - i) + i`;
  * `PerlinNoise::sample` is `samplePerlin`: the corner hashing of its
    `#else` branch, and its y fold (`yamp`, `ymin`) — the local y clamped,
    floored over the scale and subtracted after the fade weights — with the
    five-argument form and the `(0, 0)` call as the plain sample;
  * `PerlinNoise::sampleSimplex2D` and `simplexCorner` are `sampleSimplex2D`
    and `simplexGrad`, term for term;
  * `OctaveNoise::create` is `xOctaveInit`: two draws as a base, each octave
    seeded by XOR with md5("octave_<n>"), zero amplitudes skipped while the
    frequency and persistence advance;
  * `OctaveNoise::createLegacy` is `octaveInit` (with rng.h's
    `skipNextN`): its guard that the octave range be non-empty with a top
    octave of at most 0, the 262 raw generator steps skipped per octave
    above the top one, the octaves drawn and stored highest frequency
    first, the persistence 1/(2^n - 1) doubled per octave and the frequency
    2^top halved per octave;
  * `NormalNoise::create` and `NormalNoise::sample` are `xDoublePerlinInit`
    (both stacks drawn from one generator in turn, leading and trailing zero
    amplitudes trimmed before the scaling) and `sampleDoublePerlin` (the
    second stack sampled at 337/331);
  * `NormalNoise::createLegacy` is `doublePerlinInit`: two legacy stacks
    drawn in sequence from one generator, scaled by
    `(10.0 / 6.0) * n / (n + 1)` in cubiomes' order of operations.

The modern construction (all but the two legacy functions) is recorded as
adapted on the same grounds as `evaluateSpline` below, not because a
derivation was traced. Its M1 header described it as implemented from the
documented algorithms — Perlin's improved noise and the standard simplex
construction — and checked against cubiomes, but no such derivation is on
record: the commit that added it (0cbc0be) names cubiomes as its oracle and
no other source, and neither document carries the Minecraft-specific
parts. This project's own, and not cubiomes': the octave salts hashed at
run time, with no octave_-12..0 table and so no limit; the fold's 1e-7
epsilon (spec/blended-noise-spec.md); the `(sample * amplitude) *
persistence` grouping of the octave sum and the normal noise's value-factor
spelling, both measured against the server (SPEC.md section 11); and the
lattice bound, `NormalNoise::inputLimit`. lib/include/stratum/noise/perlin.hpp
declares all of it and carries the same attribution.

lib/include/stratum/rng/xoroshiro128.hpp's bounded draw,
`Xoroshiro128PlusPlus::nextInt(bound)`, is rng.h's `xNextInt`, token for
token but for its names and the threshold computed once: the low 32 bits of
a 64-bit draw multiplied by the bound, the `(uint32_t)r < n` guard, the
rejection threshold `(~n + 1) % n`, a fresh draw for each retry, and the
high half returned. The method is Lemire's published multiply-and-shift
with rejection ("Fast Random Integer Generation in an Interval", 2019), but
the paper does not say which 32 bits of a 64-bit draw to take, and nothing
but cubiomes is on record for that choice: M1 declined to write the
function for want of an oracle (dfce50c), and it arrived with perlin.cpp,
checked against cubiomes alone (0cbc0be). It is recorded as adapted on the
same grounds as `evaluateSpline` below. Not adapted: the generator, the
64-to-128-bit seed upgrade and the other draws, which M1 wrote and checked
against the JDK before cubiomes was consulted (dfce50c); cubiomes'
`xSetSeed` later confirmed the seed upgrade constant for constant.

The named-noise seeding — `XoroshiroPositionalFactory` and `seedFromHashOf`
in the same header, with the modern path of lib/src/noise_registry.cpp's
`NoiseRegistry::create` — is adapted from biomenoise.c's `setBiomeSeed` and
`init_climate_seed`: the world seed through `xSetSeed`, two `xNextLong`
draws taken once as a 128-bit base, and each noise's generator that base
XORed with the halves of md5("minecraft:<noise>"), the digest's first eight
bytes, big-endian, into the low word. It arrived in the same commit as
`evaluateSpline` (d47c53d) and is recorded on the same grounds; its
octave-level form, `xOctaveInit`, is recorded above. This project's own:
the digest computed at run time from whatever identifier a pack names
(lib/src/md5.cpp, from RFC 1321), where cubiomes carries the halves for
its climate noises' names as constants; and each noise's octaves taken from
the pack's `worldgen/noise` entry, where cubiomes hardcodes them per climate
parameter. Checked independently of cubiomes: the halves, against md5sum.
What lib/src/positional_random.cpp builds on the factory — a second fork and
the position mix of `PositionalSource` — is not cubiomes': it was recovered
against the server (SPEC.md section 11).

lib/src/noise_registry.cpp adapts the legacy Nether climate from
biomenoise.c's `setNetherSeed`: `minecraft:temperature` from
java.util.Random(worldSeed) and `minecraft:vegetation` from
java.util.Random(worldSeed + 1), each a two-octave stack pair from octave
-7, whatever parameters the pack gives. `minecraft:offset` as zero is this
project's reading of `getNetherBiome` sampling that climate unshifted, not
code adapted from it.

lib/src/density_interpreter.cpp's `Interpreter::evaluateSpline` follows
biomenoise.c's `getSpline`: the knot search, the straight line off either
end, and the cubic as `lerp(k, n, o) + k * (1 - k) * lerp(k, p, q)` with its
two tangent terms. It is recorded as an adaptation because it cannot be
shown independent of that function — it arrived in the same commit as the
copy in tools/vectors/climate_vectors.c and matches it term for term — not
because a derivation was traced. The float-throughout evaluation is this
project's own (SPEC.md section 11).

tools/vectors/climate_vectors.c carries `getSplineStrict`, a copy of
biomenoise.c's `getSpline` with its double `lerp` helper replaced by a
float one; that file's licence expression is Apache-2.0 AND MIT for it.
tools/vectors/blended_vectors.c's `initBlended` reproduces the opening of
biomenoise.c's `initSurfaceNoise`: a Java LCG seeded with the world seed,
then `octaveInit` from octave -15 with 16 octaves twice and from -7 with 8,
into the octave buffer at 0, 16 and 32; that file's licence expression is
Apache-2.0 AND MIT for it. The vector drivers otherwise call cubiomes' own
functions, built from a checkout fetched at run time.

tools/analysis/legacy-goldens-biome-analyze.cpp reproduces the same legacy
Nether climate a second time, for analysis: `setNetherSeed`, `octaveInit`
and `doublePerlinInit`. tools/analysis/legacy-goldens-surface-decoder.hpp
re-expresses `octaveInit`'s draw order as an index formula
(`octaveInitBlock`, `octaveInitLayout`); its handling of a zero amplitude is
deepslate's, observed, not cubiomes'.

lib/src/end_islands.cpp and lib/include/stratum/noise/end_islands.hpp
adapt the shape of the End's island field from biomenoise.c's
`getEndHeightNoise`, every constant of it: the central island at
100 - 8 * distance on the eight-block grid; the outer islands in the
+/-12 cells around a column's sixteen-block cell, only where that cell's
squared distance exceeds 4096 and the simplex reads below -0.9; each
island's steepness `(|x| * 3439 + |z| * 147) % 13 + 9` (the integer form,
which cubiomes keeps as a commented-out line); the offsets
`within - 2 * cell`; and the clamp to [-100, 80]. The header used to call
this "the community-documented End island field" without naming a
document; no source but cubiomes is on record, so it is recorded as an
adaptation on the same grounds as `evaluateSpline`. NOT adapted, and
measured against the server instead (SPEC.md section 11): floorDiv and
floorMod where cubiomes truncates; each island's height clamped and the
highest taken, where cubiomes takes the smallest steepness-weighted
squared distance and one square root; and the 17292-step seeding skip,
found by scoring every skip in [0, 65536] against the server's own field.
`setEndSeed` carries the same literal, which corroborates the measurement.

lib/src/biome_source.cpp and lib/include/stratum/biome/source.hpp adapt
the structure of the End's biome source from cubiomes' biomenoise.c,
`mapEnd`, `mapEndBiome` and `getEndBiome`: the chunk taken from the quart
by a floor shift; the central test, inclusive at 4096 in 64-bit chunk
coordinates; one sample per chunk, in the island-field cell holding the
chunk's centre; and the three edges with their inclusivity (cubiomes'
h < 3600, h <= 10000 and h <= 14400 on its squared island distance, which
are erosion > 0.25, >= -0.0625 and >= -0.21875). NOT adapted from
cubiomes: the order of the last two tests, which sends a NaN to
end_barrens. That order is this project's own, unmeasured choice — in
erosion terms cubiomes' own order would send a NaN to small_end_islands —
made so that a NaN lands where cubiomes puts its outer rings. cubiomes
models those rings with one overflow test, `(int)rsq < 0` on the square of
the island-field cell coordinate, in two places: `sampleNoiseColumnEnd`
makes their density column NaN and `mapEndBiome` makes their biome
end_barrens, so between them a NaN island field reads end_barrens. That
is this project's reading of cubiomes, not code adapted from it. What the
server answers differently — it reads the dimension's own router where
cubiomes evaluates a fixed copy of the island field — follows the server
(SPEC.md section 11, "The End's biome source").

